rules_version = '2';
//
// NOT DEPLOYED — and that is deliberate. Kept as a safety net, not as the
// control that protects the images.
//
// These rules only take effect if the logo bucket is ever REGISTERED WITH
// FIREBASE STORAGE. It is not, and should not be. The bucket is a plain GCS
// bucket created by Terraform with uniform bucket-level access, public access
// prevention ENFORCED, and exactly one IAM principal: the API's service
// account. The browser has no Firebase Storage client pointed at it and no
// credentials for it; images reach the page solely through short-lived signed
// URLs the API mints per request.
//
// Registering the bucket with Firebase Storage — which is what the console's
// "Get Started" button does, and what `firebase deploy --only storage` demands
// — would CREATE a client-facing surface that does not exist today, purely to
// satisfy a deploy step. That is the wrong trade: it widens the attack surface
// to make a config file deployable.
//
// So `storage` is intentionally absent from firebase.json. This file stays
// checked in so that IF anyone ever does enable Firebase Storage here, the
// default is already deny-everything rather than whatever the console
// scaffolds. See 03-DEPLOY-RUNBOOK.md.
//
// Logo images are private. They are served only via short-lived signed URLs
// minted by the API. No client reads or writes, ever.
service firebase.storage {
  match /b/{bucket}/o {
    match /{allPaths=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
